  "description": "Session-based authentication for ASP.NET Core: session creation/validation, pluggable Mongo/Postgres storage, and FluentValidation request validation. See the README for setup and usage.",
  "id": "ApiFeatures.Authentications.Sessions",
  "isPrerelease": false,
  "lastEdited": "2026-11-12T03:22:22.77Z",
  "listed": true,
  "packageHash": "PVSqa3Vo3RLIs0ZM/jFXMaljRbDvqfMim11MHHTfFUFsfqXd2swQjibMnv8JxSeqgjACzwYimRq5xWPQCA9Xsg==",
  "packageHashAlgorithm": "SHA512",
  "packageSize": 105562,
  "published": "2026-11-12T03:19:14Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "ApiFeatures.Authentications.Sessions 9.2.0 contains BREAKING CHANGES to session location configuration: ResponseLocation and the SessionResponseLocation, BodySessionResponseLocation, CookieSessionResponseLocation and SessionResponseLocationTypes types are removed; SessionLocation is now the single setting for both how create-session returns the session and where requests are read from (see \"Migrating from 9.1 to 9.2\" in the README). SessionAuthenticationSchemeOptions.SessionLocation is now nullable with no default, so code that assigns o.SessionLocation.Name throws NullReferenceException, and a handler scheme now enforces the module Scheme prefix on the Authorization header. New: cookie Domain, Path, Partitioned and ExpiryTime, startup validation, idempotent cookie logout, and an opt-in Origin (CSRF) check. 9.2.0 adds a .NET 10 (net10.0) build; .NET 9 is still supported. It no longer depends on Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi; reference it directly if your host uses AddOpenApi/MapOpenApi. Also BREAKING in 9.2.0: ISessionFeatureBusinessLogic.IsAuthenticationValidAsync now takes an AuthenticationRequestContext (username, password, device, browser, read-only client metadata, HttpContext) instead of (username, password); ISessionService gains GetUnexpiredByUsernameAsync (custom implementations must add it); SessionInfoDto is removed (use SessionSummaryDto, which never contains a session key); CreateSessionPayloadDtoValidator takes SessionMetadataLimitOptions. The MongoDatabases package now owns the BSON class maps of SessionEntity and SessionMetadata (a class map registered elsewhere makes the first use of SessionDatabaseContext fail with a TypeInitializationException) and no longer registers a process-wide Guid serializer (code that relied on it for its own Guid values gets the driver 3.x \"GuidRepresentation is Unspecified\" error and must register its own serializer). New: create-session metadata is validated against configurable limits (MetadataLimits, 400 when exceeded) and stored with the session; GET /api/sessions lists the caller's unexpired sessions without keys. Client metadata is visible to the user, so do not put secrets in it. For MongoDB create the recommended indexes { Username: 1, CreatedTime: -1 } and unique { Key: 1 }.",
